# Part II — Dead code detection, visualization, and removal

A repeatable process for finding, visualizing, and safely removing dead
code in any repo, regardless of language. "Unreferenced by a tool" is a
candidate, not a verdict — this exists because static analysis alone
produces false positives (reflection, dynamic dispatch, string-based
routing) and false negatives (partially dead branches, feature-flagged
code). Nothing gets deleted on a single signal.

## 10. Ground rules

1. **Two tracks, not one.** Static reference analysis (Track A) finds what
   looks unused. Runtime verification (Track B) confirms it's actually
   never executed. Only both tracks agreeing — or Track A plus a clean
   manual check — earns "high confidence."
2. **Confidence tiers, not a binary.** Every candidate is High, Medium, or
   Excluded. Only High gets deleted directly.
3. **Small, reviewable diffs.** Never one giant purge PR. Group by
   module/tool/confidence tier.
4. **Some things are never auto-deleted**, regardless of tooling output —
   see section 14.
5. **This is a cadence, not a one-time cleanup.** Dead code accumulates
   continuously; re-run on a schedule (section 18).

## 11. Dead-code workflow

1. **Identify the stack.** Detect languages/build systems from manifest
   files at the repo root: `package.json`, `pyproject.toml` /
   `requirements.txt`, `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml`, `pom.xml` / `build.gradle`,
   `Gemfile`, `composer.json`, `*.csproj`, `pubspec.yaml`, `Package.swift`.
   A repo can have more than one stack — run the matching tools for each.
2. **Run static analysis (Track A)** using the tool matrix in section 12.
3. **Cross-check with a full-text search**, not just the tool's AST view.
   Grep the exact symbol name across the *entire* repo — including
   templates, YAML/JSON config, IaC (Terraform/Helm/CloudFormation), and
   docs — since string-based dynamic references (DI containers, route
   tables, plugin registries, serialization keys) won't show up in an
   AST-based tool but will show up as a string literal.
4. **Run the test suite with coverage (Track B).** Anything a tool flags
   as unused *and* shows 0% coverage across the full suite moves toward
   High confidence.
5. **Check production telemetry if available** (APM traces, access logs,
   error logs) over a representative window (30–90 days, or a full
   reporting cycle for cyclical code). This catches admin tools, cron
   jobs, and rarely-hit paths that tests and grep both miss. For JVM
   specifically, prefer a runtime tool over guessing — see Scavenger in
   section 12.
6. **Categorize every candidate:**
   - **High confidence** — unreferenced by the tool, zero test coverage,
     zero string/dynamic references, zero telemetry hits, not a public
     API, not on the exclusion list (section 14).
   - **Medium confidence** — unreferenced by the tool but with partial
     coverage, referenced only from tests, or a public export with unclear
     external consumers. Mark and track, don't delete.
   - **Excluded** — matches section 14. Never touch, regardless of
     tooling.
7. **Remove High-confidence items first**, one module or tool-category per
   PR, referencing the tool output and cross-checks in the description.
8. **For Medium confidence**, mark with a tracked TODO/issue and revisit at
   the next scheduled sweep. Promote to High after two consecutive sweeps
   with no new references.
9. **Verify after every removal PR**: full test suite green, build green,
   lints green, grep confirms zero remaining references, staging deploy +
   smoke test where applicable, and a named rollback path.
10. **Generate the graphical report** (section 15) and attach it to the PR
    or sweep issue so reviewers see the blast radius, not just a list.
11. **Repeat on cadence** — see section 18.

## 12. Tool matrix by category

### JavaScript & TypeScript

- **Knip** — the strongest all-rounder: finds unused files, unexported
  functions/types/variables, and "zombie" dependencies listed in
  `package.json` across the whole project graph.
- **dead-code-checker** (`npx dead-code-checker -f ./src`) — scans source
  directories and reports unused functions/variables with file/line
  locations and bundle size impact.
- **ts-prune** — narrower, unused-exports-only; a quick second opinion
  against Knip.
- **depcheck** — unused/missing npm dependencies specifically.
- **madge** / **dependency-cruiser** — graph engines used for the visual
  report in section 15 (they surface orphan modules — files nothing
  imports — directly).

### Python

- **Vulture** — targets uncalled functions, unused attributes, and dead
  variables; attaches confidence scores per finding to reduce accidental
  deletions.
- **Pylint** — a thorough standard linter that also flags unreachable
  code and unused/unreferenced variables (`W0612`, `W0101`).
- **unimport** — unused-imports specifically, fast, autofix-capable.
- **deptry** — unused/missing dependencies in `pyproject.toml`.

### Java / Kotlin / JVM

- **Scavenger** (Naver, `naver/scavenger`) — a **runtime** dead-code
  analysis tool for JVM languages, successor to codekvast. It instruments
  a running application (no source changes), collects real method
  invocations in production, and shows a dashboard of code that's never
  actually called — this is Track B for JVM, and the most reliable way to
  catch dead code hidden behind reflection, Spring DI, or dynamic proxies,
  which static tools routinely miss.
- **PMD** (`UnusedPrivateField`, `UnusedLocalVariable`) and **detekt**
  (Kotlin, `UnusedPrivateMember`) — static, fast, good as a first pass.
- **SonarQube** — repo-wide static findings plus historical trend
  tracking across sweeps.

### Mobile frameworks

- **dead_code_analyzer** (Dart/Flutter, on pub.dev) — CLI that maps
  cross-file references to find unreferenced classes, functions, and
  variables inside active Flutter widget trees, with usage-frequency
  tracking and exclusion patterns.
- **DCM** (`dcm check-unused-code`) — commercial-grade Flutter/Dart code
  quality tool with a dedicated unused-code check, good for CI gating.
- **Periphery** (Swift/iOS) — purpose-built unused-code scanner that
  understands Swift's protocol conformance and dynamic dispatch, which
  naive AST scans get wrong.

### Enterprise & compile-time safety (C / C++ and safety-critical stacks)

- **Polyspace** (MathWorks) — advanced static analysis with data-flow
  tracing, used to prove code paths align with functional-safety standards
  like ISO 26262 (automotive) and DO-178 (aerospace). Heavier-weight than
  the tools below, but appropriate where dead code in a safety-critical
  path is a certification issue, not just tech debt.
- **cppcheck** — lighter-weight static analysis, unused-function/variable
  detection, good default for non-safety-critical C/C++.
- **include-what-you-use (IWYU)** — finds unused `#include`s specifically.
- Compiler flags `-Wunused-function -Wunused-variable` — free, always on.

### Go

- **codecoroner** — detects unused functions and variables specifically.
- **`golang.org/x/tools/cmd/deadcode`** — official tool, whole-program
  reachability analysis from `main`.
- **staticcheck** (`U1000`) / **golangci-lint** (`unused`) — fast,
  CI-friendly.

### Rust

- **cargo-udeps** — unused dependencies.
- Built-in `#[warn(dead_code)]` lint + **cargo clippy** — unused code
  inside the crate.

### Ruby

- **debride** — static dead-method detection.
- **coverband** — production runtime usage tracking (Track B for Ruby,
  same role Scavenger plays for JVM).

### PHP

- **PHPStan** / **Psalm** — dead-code detection rule sets.
- **Rector** — can both detect and auto-refactor dead code.

### .NET

- **endjin/deadcode** — .NET Global Tool for detecting dead code, notably
  tuned for the kind of unused scaffolding AI code generators tend to
  leave behind.
- Roslyn analyzers `IDE0051`/`IDE0052` (unused private members), `IDE0005`
  (unused usings) — built into the compiler, zero setup.

### SQL / schema

- No dedicated "dead code" tool — cross-reference ORM models against the
  live schema, and check `pg_stat_statements` / slow-query logs for tables
  and columns with zero reads over a full reporting cycle.

### Generic fallback (no tool for the language/framework)

1. Build a symbol inventory: every top-level function, class, exported
   name.
2. `ripgrep` (`rg -w '<symbol>'`) the exact name across the whole repo,
   excluding its own definition line.
3. Zero-hit symbols are candidates — manually check for that language's
   dynamic-usage patterns (reflection, string routing tables, ORM magic
   methods, event/hook registries, template interpolation) before trusting
   a zero-hit result.
4. Cross-reference with coverage data if any exists.

## 13. Confirming production telemetry without an APM

If there's no APM/tracing tool (and no Scavenger/coverband-style runtime
agent), approximate Track B:

- Grep application logs for the function/route/class name over a wide
  window.
- For web endpoints: check load balancer / web server access logs for the
  route.
- For scheduled jobs: check the scheduler's execution history.
- For CLI commands: check shell history, internal docs, or support
  tickets referencing the command.

Absence of a hit here is weaker evidence than a runtime agent trace, but
combined with zero static references and zero test coverage it still
counts.

## 14. Never auto-delete these, regardless of tool output

- Public/exported library or API surface without a formal deprecation
  cycle and version bump.
- Anything referenced only from IaC, CI/CD pipeline config, cron
  schedulers, or message-queue consumer registrations — invisible to
  code-level static analysis.
- Migration files and schema history.
- Code gated behind a feature flag that's merely off right now — check the
  flag system's current state, not just the code.
- Generated code (protobuf/gRPC stubs, GraphQL codegen, ORM models,
  OpenAPI clients) — fix the generator/source-of-truth, not the output.
- Anything modified in the last N days (e.g. 14) without a full-team
  review.

## 15. Graphical dead-code reports

A list of file paths is hard to review; a graph of what's dead, what's
uncertain, and what depends on what is not. Two ways to get one:

### 15a. Language-native graph tools

These render real import/call graphs and are the fastest path when the
tool exists for your stack:

| Language | Command | Output |
|---|---|---|
| JS/TS | `npx madge --image graph.svg src/` or `npx dependency-cruiser --output-type dot src \| dot -T svg -o graph.svg` | Module graph; `dependency-cruiser` can flag orphans directly |
| Python | `pydeps mypackage --show-deps -o graph.svg` | Import graph |
| Go | `go-callvis ./...` | Call graph, SVG |
| Java | `jdeps --dot-output ./out com.example.App && dot -Tsvg out/*.dot -o graph.svg` | Package/class dependency graph |
| Rust | `cargo modules generate graph \| dot -Tsvg -o graph.svg` | Module graph |
| Ruby | `debride --graph` (or export findings into 15b) | Method reference graph |

Color/annotate the output: any node these tools mark as an orphan/unused
maps directly to a "dead" node in the unified report below.

### 15b. Unified generator (works for any language, ties into the workflow)

Since not every stack has a graph tool, and reviewers want one consistent
report regardless of language, use the included generator script,
[`scripts/dead_code_graph.py`](./scripts/dead_code_graph.py). It reads a
standardized findings file and renders a single self-contained HTML report
with:

- A Graphviz dependency graph — dead (High confidence) nodes in red,
  Medium confidence in amber, live nodes in the default color.
- A bar chart of dead lines of code by module/top-level directory.
- A pie chart of confidence-tier distribution across the whole sweep.

**Input schema** (`dead_code_findings.json`) — this is the same
categorization produced by section 11, step 6, just serialized:

```json
{
  "nodes": [
    { "id": "src/utils/date.ts", "label": "date.ts", "status": "dead", "lines": 42 },
    { "id": "src/utils/string.ts", "label": "string.ts", "status": "live", "lines": 120 },
    { "id": "src/legacy/old.ts", "label": "old.ts", "status": "medium", "lines": 30 }
  ],
  "edges": [
    { "from": "src/index.ts", "to": "src/utils/string.ts" },
    { "from": "src/index.ts", "to": "src/legacy/old.ts" }
  ]
}
```

`status` is one of `dead` (High confidence), `medium`, or `live`/omitted.
Populate `nodes`/`lines` from whichever tool in section 12 you ran;
populate `edges` from that tool's import graph if it exposes one (Knip,
madge, and `go-callvis` all do), or omit edges entirely for a flat report
if a graph isn't available — the bar/pie charts still render.

**Run it:**

```bash
pip install graphviz matplotlib --break-system-packages   # one-time
python3 scripts/dead_code_graph.py dead_code_findings.json report.html
```

Open `report.html` — it's a single file, safe to attach to a PR or drop in
a shared drive, no server required. A worked example ships alongside the
script: `scripts/dead_code_findings.sample.json` in, `scripts/dead_code_report.sample.html` out.

## 16. Wiring dead-code detection into an AI agent

If you want an agent (Copilot, Claude, etc.) to run this rather than doing
it by hand, instruct it explicitly to:

1. Detect the stack per section 11 and actually **run** the matching
   tool(s) from section 12 via the terminal — not just read source and
   guess.
2. Perform the Track B cross-checks in section 11 steps 4–5 / section 13
   before proposing any deletion.
3. Never touch anything matching section 14's exclusion list.
4. Categorize every finding per section 11 step 6, writing results into
   the `dead_code_findings.json` schema from section 15b as it goes.
5. Run `scripts/dead_code_graph.py` on the resulting file and attach
   `report.html` to the sweep's summary.
6. Only remove High-confidence items directly; Medium-confidence items get
   marked and tracked, never deleted outright.

This is a different job from the codebase sweep protocol in Part I,
section 7: that protocol simplifies code that's still used but
over-engineered; this one removes code nothing uses at all. Run this
workflow first — it shrinks what the ladder sweep has to consider.

## 17. Dead-code PR verification checklist

- [ ] Full test suite passes.
- [ ] Build passes.
- [ ] Lints pass.
- [ ] Grep confirms zero remaining references to removed symbols/files.
- [ ] PR description states which tool(s) flagged it and which confidence
      tier it was.
- [ ] `report.html` (section 15) attached or linked.
- [ ] Staging deploy + smoke test completed, if applicable.
- [ ] Rollback path is a plain `git revert` (no follow-up migrations
      needed to undo).

## 18. Cadence and ownership

- Run a full dead-code sweep quarterly, and always before a major version
  bump or a large refactor.
- Assign one owner per sweep who signs off on the final report and graph.
- Keep a running "Medium confidence" backlog (tracking label or project
  board column) instead of leaving borderline calls undecided indefinitely.
- Track a simple metric over time — lines removed, files removed,
  dependencies dropped — to show the sweep is paying for itself.
